DATABASE LAYER (Worker required)
- Run sql.js inside a Web Worker behind a typed DbClient (init, getSchemaContext, dryRun, execute) with a typed message protocol: request IDs, explicit success/error responses, an init state, pending requests rejected when a worker is terminated, stale responses ignored. dryRun and execute accept ONLY the branded type ValidatedSql, which only sqlValidator can create. Execution timeout 5 seconds: on timeout terminate the worker, re-create it (fresh deterministic seed), and report "Query timed out". "Reset database" re-runs the full setup sequence below.
- If a Worker cannot be created in this environment, show a clear error and keep query execution DISABLED (no main-thread fallback for SQL) and tell me in your reply.
- Connection setup order inside the Worker: create database -> PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON -> create tables -> seed -> PRAGMA foreign_key_check (must return no rows) -> TRUSTED SEED VALIDATION -> load schema context -> PRAGMA query_only = ON (defense in depth only; SQLite documents that the database is not truly read-only under it).
- TRUSTED INTERNAL SQL: SQL written in code by the application itself (creating tables, seeding, loading the schema, seed validation) is trusted, is REQUIRED to run in Stage 1, and never passes through sqlValidator. "No SQL execution" in this spec always means: no execution of user-submitted or LLM-generated SQL.
- TRUSTED SEED VALIDATION: private Worker routines that run trusted internal SQL, are not reachable through DbClient, and check the coverage, date-integrity, foreign-key and distinguishability guarantees below. The init response returns a seedReport (named checks with pass/fail and details) that the Self-Tests panel displays.
- Stage 1 scope: init, seeding, trusted seed validation and schema retrieval are operational. dryRun and execute must throw "not available until Stage 2". Do NOT implement execution of user-submitted or LLM-generated SQL in Stage 1.

SAMPLE DATABASE (deterministic, seeded with a fixed-seed generator)
About 40 customers, 25 products, 300 orders, 700 order_items. Dates are stored as 'YYYY-MM-DD' text from the LOCAL calendar date (see dateHelpers). Order dates span from the first day of the month 11 months before the current month through today.
DDL with declared foreign keys:
- customers(customer_id PK, name, email, city, state, signup_date, segment)
- products(product_id PK, name, category, price, cost)
- orders(order_id PK, customer_id FK->customers, status, created_at, shipped_at, delivered_at, discount_pct)
- order_items(item_id PK, order_id FK->orders, product_id FK->products, quantity, unit_price)
- returns(return_id PK, order_id FK->orders, reason, returned_at)
Semantics: created_at always set; shipped_at NULL for pending and cancelled; delivered_at NULL unless status is delivered. unit_price is the list price BEFORE discount; discount_pct is an order-level percentage (0-100) applied to all items of that order. Returns exist only for delivered orders. A "returned order" is an order with at least one row in returns. There is NO 'returned' status.
Date integrity (checked by seed validation): no generated date is later than today; for every order created_at <= shipped_at <= delivered_at whenever those values are present; returned_at is on or after the order's delivered_at and not later than today.
Coverage guarantees (all intervals half-open [start, end)): each of the 11 complete calendar months before the current month has at least 5 non-cancelled orders by created_at; the current (partial) calendar month has at least 3 non-cancelled orders with created_at on or before today; the last complete month AND the last complete quarter each have at least 10 non-cancelled orders by created_at and at least 3 orders with delivered_at inside the interval; at least 15 orders have created_at in [today-29, today+1); every status value occurs; at least 10 orders have returns. The generator and the seed validation implement exactly this rule.
Distinguishability guarantees: total revenue for the last complete month differs between created_at-based and delivered_at-based filtering; the top 3 customers by revenue differ from the top 3 by order count; revenue, net revenue and revenue before discounts all differ for the last complete quarter.

TRUSTED CONTEXT (static objects in code, built by /engine/schemaLoader.ts and /engine/context.ts; never passed through the SQL validator)
- SCHEMA: tables, columns, types, PK/FK from sqlite_master / PRAGMA table_info / PRAGMA foreign_key_list (excluding sqlite_ tables), plus JOIN_HINTS: orders.customer_id = customers.customer_id; order_items.order_id = orders.order_id; order_items.product_id = products.product_id; returns.order_id = orders.order_id; plus short column descriptions written in code.
- ENUM_VALUES (controlled vocabularies, defined in code, NOT queried from rows): orders.status in pending, shipped, delivered, cancelled; customers.segment in Consumer, Corporate, Small Business; products.category in Electronics, Clothing, Home, Books, Sports; returns.reason in defective, wrong_item, not_needed, late_delivery. All other text columns (customers.name, city, state; products.name) are FREE-FORM.
- GLOSSARY:
  * revenue = SUM(order_items.quantity * order_items.unit_price * (1 - orders.discount_pct / 100.0)) over orders with status <> 'cancelled'. Returned orders still count.
  * revenue before discounts = the same without the discount factor.
  * net revenue = revenue excluding orders that have any row in returns.
  * returned order = an order with at least one row in returns.
  * active customer = at least one non-cancelled order created in the last 90 days.
  * DATE BASIS: (1) A status word combined with a period ("delivered orders last month", "shipped orders in March") applies the period to that status's own timestamp: delivered -> delivered_at, shipped -> shipped_at (cancelled and pending use created_at). (2) Revenue questions with no status or timing word default to orders.created_at. (3) Return-timing words use returns.returned_at. (4) Order counts, listings and other order metrics that have a period or time grouping but no stated date column and no status word have NO default date: that is ambiguous.
  * "Top N" defaults to 10 only when the ranking metric is clear.
  * Totals and rankings for revenue, order counts or customer counts with no time wording are answered over all time (state it as an assumption).
- SENSITIVE_COLUMNS: customers.email (the designated sensitive column; claims are limited to designated columns).
- /engine/dateHelpers.ts (deterministic): uses the browser's LOCAL calendar. Compute every date from local year/month/day components; NEVER use toISOString or UTC conversion for date strings. Accept an injected "now" for tests. Outputs (YYYY-MM-DD): today, yesterday, week_start (Monday), month_start, last_month_start, last_month_end_exclusive, quarter_start, last_quarter_start, last_quarter_end_exclusive, year_start, last_year_start, and the dates 7, 30 and 90 days ago. Test month/year boundaries, leap-year February and local midnight.

SQL SAFETY ENGINE (/engine/sqlValidator.ts; FAILS CLOSED)
SCOPE: applies to ANY untrusted SQL string: SQL written by the LLM and SQL pasted into the Validator Playground. Trusted internal SQL (seeding, schema loading, seed validation) never passes through it. The validator returns a ValidatedSql value only when steps 1-8 pass; step 9 (dry run) and step 10 (execution) accept only ValidatedSql. In the Validator Playground (validation-only mode) run steps 1-9 and NEVER step 10: pasted SQL is never executed.
Run these steps in order; any failure rejects the query with a clear validation error that can trigger repair:
1. Parse the COMPLETE statement into an AST. A parse failure or inconclusive result rejects. NEVER fall back to a token scanner to approve a query.
2. Exactly one statement, and the terminal executed statement is a SELECT. CTEs are allowed only if the final statement is a SELECT (a WITH prefix alone proves nothing).
3. Reject star projections (SELECT * and t.*). COUNT(*) is allowed. Reject window functions (OVER) and RECURSIVE.
4. A top-level LIMIT is required and must be at most 1000.
5. Function allowlist: COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, ROUND, ABS, COALESCE, IFNULL, NULLIF, CAST, LENGTH, LOWER, UPPER, TRIM, SUBSTR, DATE, STRFTIME, JULIANDAY, plus CASE expressions. Any other function rejects.
6. Every table exists in the schema (CTE names defined in the query are allowed); every column resolves against schema metadata (an unqualified column must exist in exactly one in-scope table); at most 6 tables joined.
7. Any identifier equal to a sensitive column name (normalized: lower-cased, quotes/backticks/brackets stripped) anywhere, qualified or not, including aliases and subqueries, rejects.
8. Whole-token checks outside string literals reject INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, DROP, ALTER, CREATE, TRUNCATE, REPLACE INTO, MERGE, ATTACH, DETACH, PRAGMA, VACUUM and any sqlite_ object. The column name created_at must not be mis-flagged (whole tokens only). The scalar function replace() is NOT on the function allowlist, so it is rejected by step 5 (as a disallowed function), not by the write-keyword rule. REPLACE INTO is rejected as a write. These token checks are an ADDITIONAL filter and never authorize anything.
9. Dry run EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN must succeed (only to confirm SQLite accepts the statement; never parse its output).
10. Execute in the Worker; read at most 1000 rows while stepping (never edit the SQL text); 5 second timeout. Zero rows is a valid result: show "No rows matched". The engine never claims data does not exist.

JSON CONTRACT (single source of truth; EXACT key names in config, types and UI; never rename or add keys)
interface VimarshResponse { status: "SUCCESS"|"CLARIFY"|"REJECT"; reasoning: string; sql_query: string|null; follow_up_question: string|null; clarification_options: string[]; ambiguity_type: string|null; assumptions: string[]; tables_used: string[]; confidence: number; rejection_reason: string|null; }
Parsing: trim, remove at most one surrounding Markdown code fence, then JSON.parse the ENTIRE remaining text inside try/catch. Reject extra text, multiple objects, truncated output, empty or safety-blocked responses (check the finish reason); no brace scanning. Read response text using whatever the installed SDK provides (property or method).
/engine/responseValidator.ts validates at runtime: exactly these 10 keys, correct types, status enum, confidence in 0..1, ambiguity_type null or comma-separated codes from A1-A11, clarification_options unique non-empty strings (2-4 for CLARIFY, [] otherwise), tables_used entries exist in the schema, and status rules: SUCCESS has non-empty sql_query and null follow_up_question and null rejection_reason; CLARIFY has null sql_query, non-empty follow_up_question, null rejection_reason; REJECT has null sql_query and follow_up_question and non-empty rejection_reason. In REPAIR mode CLARIFY is invalid. On any failure retry once with a "return valid JSON matching the contract only" nudge, then show a neutral "Vimarsh could not process this request" card. The UI shows tables derived from the validated SQL's AST and ignores tables_used if it disagrees. Label confidence "model self-rating"; never use it for decisions.

CONVERSATION STATE
- The UI keeps the full timeline (including result tables). Only /engine/historySerializer.ts decides what reaches the LLM. It serializes the most recent 6 PRIOR turns (never the current message) plus the pinned original question of a pending clarification: user turns as their question text; assistant turns as short summaries: SUCCESS -> "answered; sql: ...; assumptions: ...", CLARIFY -> the question and options, REJECT -> "rejected". It NEVER includes result rows, cell values, table exports, chart data or the API key.
- state.pending = null | { original_question, round }. A CLARIFY response sets pending (keeps original_question if already set, increments round); SUCCESS or REJECT sets pending = null. A "New question" button clears it, and while pending the input hint reads "Answer, or click New question to ask something else". Chips are continuations by definition; typed text while pending is treated as a continuation by the app and judged by the model (documented limitation).
- In code: if round is already 3 and the model returns CLARIFY, convert it to a REJECT that names the details to specify and asks the user to rephrase.

PROMPT ASSEMBLY
/engine/systemPrompt.ts exports SYSTEM_PROMPT_TEMPLATE and PROMPT_VERSION (I will paste the text in Stage 3; create a clear placeholder now). Any behavior-changing edit of the prompt must increment PROMPT_VERSION. promptBuilder.ts fills ONLY these placeholders: {{CURRENT_DATE}}, {{DATE_HELPERS}}, {{DB_DIALECT}} = "SQLite", {{SCHEMA}}, {{ENUM_VALUES}}, {{GLOSSARY}}, {{SENSITIVE_COLUMNS}}; the result goes in systemInstruction.
The USER message contains exactly these tags, built from code:
<mode>ANSWER or REPAIR</mode>
<pending_clarification>true or false</pending_clarification>
<clarification_round>number</clarification_round>
<conversation_history>serialized prior turns</conversation_history>
<user_question>the latest user turn</user_question>
REPAIR adds <error_source>VALIDATION or EXECUTION</error_source>, <failed_sql>, <db_error>, with the same history and user_question. Never send an error as the user question.
SAFE SERIALIZATION for EVERY untrusted field (history, user_question, failed_sql, db_error): first apply length bounds (user question 500 chars, each history entry 500, failed_sql 2000, db_error 500), then replace "<" with "&lt;" and ">" with "&gt;" so user text can never create a tag. The UI keeps and displays the original text; only the serialized copy is escaped.
/engine/inputGuard.ts: trim and length-limit; a DETERMINISTIC hard reject (no LLM call, no query) ONLY for clear instruction-override or exfiltration phrases (for example "ignore previous/all/your instructions or rules", "reveal/show your system prompt", "you are now", "developer mode", "jailbreak", "act as admin"). It must NOT reject benign words like "drop", "delete" or "update" in ordinary analytical questions (unit-test both directions). If anything is detected, reject the WHOLE request, even when a legitimate question is mixed in.

PIPELINE AND BUDGETS
inputGuard -> historySerializer + promptBuilder -> llmClient -> strict parse -> responseValidator -> (SUCCESS) sqlValidator -> dry run -> Worker execute -> results. Independent budgets: transport retries (HTTP 429/503) at most 2 retries with exponential backoff, honoring Retry-After; JSON-format retry exactly 1; SQL repair at most 2 repair attempts (so at most 3 executions), each re-validated by the full validator. A per-question deadline of 60 seconds. Stop immediately on cancellation or non-retryable errors. Disable send while a request is pending; ignore superseded responses. A repaired result shows a "repaired" badge with the original error.

AUDIT LOG (/engine/auditLog.ts)
Structured events with a source enum: INPUT_GUARD, MODEL, RESPONSE_VALIDATOR, SQL_VALIDATOR, EXECUTOR, REPAIR, RETRY_EXHAUSTED. Fields: timestamp, PROMPT_VERSION, model name, mode, status, source, validator result, row count, latency, repair count, and the failed and final SQL (each truncated to 2000 chars). Never record the API key, result rows, raw prompts or raw API error bodies. README note: SQL may contain literals the user typed. "Download audit log (JSON)" button.

